<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ekofisk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ekofisk oil field</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ekofisk is an oil field in the Norwegian sector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, discovered by Phillips Petroleum in 1969 and in production from 1971. The first major petroleum find on the Norwegian continental shelf, it opened the way to an oil and gas industry that transformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> into one of the world's richest countries and funds its sovereign wealth fund.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ekofisk.jpg" alt="Ekofisk" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gautier, D.L. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_Sea_Fields_Zoom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ekofisk lies in block 2/4 of the Norwegian sector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, about 320 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavanger">Stavanger</a>. The field is a north–south anticline with a central graben, covering 49 square kilometres, formed by the movement of Permian Zechstein salt. Its oil lies at a depth of about 3 km in the Paleocene Ekofisk Formation and the Upper Cretaceous Tor Formation, true chalks made of the skeletal remains of coccolithophores, with porosities of 30 to 40 per cent. The source rocks are Upper Jurassic Kimmeridgian shales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Interest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> had been stirred by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> gas field, and Ekofisk was the first discovery of oil after more than 200 exploration <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> had been drilled there. The structure was found with seismic reflection data in the 1960s, though gas in the overlying rocks at first distorted the readings. In 1971 Phillips began producing directly to tankers from four subsea wells.</p><p>In April 1977 a blowout at the Ekofisk Bravo platform, caused by a wrongly installed downhole safety valve, released an estimated 80,000 to 126,000 barrels, the largest blowout in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Red Adair and his crew helped cap it.</p><p>In the mid-1980s the seabed and platforms were found to be sinking. As oil was drawn out, pore pressure in the chalk fell and the rock compacted; water was injected to restore the pressure, but water-saturated chalk proved weaker still, and the total subsidence was calculated at almost 6 m by the end of Phillips's concession. Pressed by the Norwegian government, Phillips engaged the French company Technip. Five interconnected platforms were raised together by extending their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> legs, using 108 hydraulic cylinders, in a lift completed on 17 August 1987 and recorded by Guinness World Records as the largest jack-up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Greater Ekofisk Area includes the Cod, West Ekofisk, Tor, Albuskjell, Eldfisk, Embla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a> fields. The Ekofisk Center, a complex of 29 platforms, also serves as a transport hub for surrounding fields. Its oil goes by the Norpipe pipeline to the Teesside Refinery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and its gas by the Norpipe gas pipeline to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ekofisk oil field” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early modern period timber exports, silver from Kongsberg (from 1623) and copper from Røros (from 1644) added new wealth, and since the discovery of the <b>Ekofisk</b> field in 1969 oil and gas have made Norway one of the richest countries in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
